  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14226856/assets/873c7f6b6e036f592f693bf2d20e1980b890f888d08af40b775cb77deba07a76.png)\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the parallelogram $ABCD?$",
  "worked_solution": "The diagonal $\\overset{―}{BD}$ divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles. Therefore, the area of the parallelogram is twice the area of $△ABD$ (see the illustration).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14226856/assets/7572dac85d8e6029b52d199bdfcb87b4a92ffafc008fe999c10848153f9f67d8.png)\n\n Computing the area of $△ABD,$ we find\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}(10)(6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{38}^{∘} \\\\  & =30\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{38}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈18.470,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & =2{A}_{ABD} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 18.470 \\\\  & =36.940.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram is $37.$",
